      title: "Power as a character's sole motivation",
      metadata: {
        type: "craft",
        authorVetted: true,
        documentType: "pattern",
        family: "general",
        detectability: "model-assisted",
        scopes: [
          "manuscript"
        ],
        genres: [
          "general-fiction",
          "progression-fantasy",
          "litrpg"
        ],
        subgenres: [],
        exclusions: [
          "dungeon-core",
          "base-building"
        ],
        modifiers: [
          "dungeon-core: Growth is legitimately the protagonist's condition and situation in this subgenre — an immobile core's central drive being \"grow stronger\" is not the same failure this pattern names. Look instead for whether the core has any want beyond growth once growth stops being urgent.",
          "base-building: The protagonist's goal is often legitimately communal (the settlement's survival and flourishing) rather than personal power; apply this pattern to individual cast members' motivations, not to the collective goal itself."
        ],
        tags: [
          "character",
          "motivation",
          "progression"
        ],
        aliases: [
          "motivation monotony",
          "getting-stronger-and-nothing-else"
        ],
        sourceConfidence: "medium",
        citations: [
          {
            id: "src.internal.litrpg-craft-failures-research",
            label: "LitRPG Craft-Failure Pattern Research (Part A–D)"
          },
          {
            id: "src.book.cron-story-genius",
            label: "Story Genius: How to Use Brain Science to Go Beyond Outlining"
          }
        ]
      },
      section: "Original micro-examples",
      content: "*Monotonous:* Every internal-narration paragraph in a five-chapter stretch\nends on some form of \"I have to get stronger.\" No other want is stated or\nimplied, and no choice in the stretch trades power against anything else.\n\n*Instrumental:* The same character wants strength specifically to return to\na hometown before a debt collector seizes it, and one scene has them turn\ndown a faster path to power because it would mean leaving too late to make\nthe deadline in person — power is in service of something the reader can\nname without the word \"power.\"",
