      section: "When this advice does not apply",
      content: "- A story whose explicit premise is that power *can* solve everything (a\n  wish-fulfillment or satire piece examining that idea directly) is using\n  the absence of negative space on purpose.\n- Not every problem needs to be permanently unsolvable — a negative-space\n  list can shrink over a series as the story earns a resolution, provided the\n  resolution is dramatized rather than assumed.\n- Early volumes of a planned long series may deliberately leave the list\n  large and mostly untouched, with payoff planned for a later book.",
