      section: "Why readers may care",
      content: "Two independent lines of evidence point at the same idea from different\nangles. Reader-side analysis of long-running series names inconsistent\npromise-keeping — a cozy opening that turns grim, or a desperate-stakes\nopening that goes soft — as a throughline in series that lose readers over\ntime. Separately, early-chapter retention research treats poor chapter-one\nto chapter-two retention as a promise mismatch rather than a prose-quality\nproblem: readers aren't leaving because chapter two is badly written, they're\nleaving because chapter two isn't the book chapter one promised. Promise\nconsistency is the concept that connects a scene-level craft note to a\nseries-level retention outcome.",
